The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
sum = inet_frag_rbtree_purge(&q->rb_fragments, reason);
sub_frag_mem_limit(q->fqdir, sum);
</text>
<text>
if (!(q->flags & INET_FRAG_COMPLETE)) {
    struct sk_buff *prev = q->fragments_tail;
    // subsequent use of prev (e.g., FRAG_CB(prev)) after flush
}
</text>
Reason:
The first statement frees all skbs in the fragment queue but leaves `q->rb_fragments`, `q->fragments_tail`, and `q->last_run_head` pointing to freed memory. The second statement shows the concurrent fragment code that, after acquiring the queue lock, checks `INET_FRAG_COMPLETE` which is not set by the flush. It then dereferences `q->fragments_tail`, leading to a use-after-free. The missing reset of the pointers allows this stale reference to be accessed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
q->rb_fragments = RB_ROOT;
q->fragments_tail = NULL;
q->last_run_head = NULL;
</text>
Reason:
These three lines reset the queue’s pointers after purging the skbs. By setting `rb_fragments` to `RB_ROOT` and `fragments_tail`/`last_run_head` to NULL, the flushed queue now appears empty. Any concurrent fragment that resumes after the flush will find an empty queue (e.g., `fragments_tail == NULL`) and start a new reassembly run instead of using the freed skbs, eliminating the use-after-free.

